A||||8||fiero56||Stumbling||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||10-16-2000||10:15 AM||||Just got an '86SE with 100,000 miles. The car sat parked for a year, and wouldn't start when it went up for sale. I bought it and gave it a good tune up. It now idles perfectly. But the car stumbles badly upon any throttle inputs. I have replaced plugs, wires, cap, rotor, tach filter, IAC, TPS, MAP, fixed all vaccume leaks, checked EGR tube for breaks, checked timing, and it still stumbles terribly, on the edge of backfiring. No codes stored either. <P>Any ideas as to what could be the culprit?||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||10-16-2000||10:46 AM||||Check fuel pressure. check the line to the MAP again. make sure it's not blocked/pinched. (MAP is used to adjust timing among other things.)<P>What method did you use to set timing?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tigger||10-16-2000||10:54 AM||||Check the pressure if you have a gauge like ogre says.  Fuel filter might be clogged, I'd start with replacing that.  If that doesn't work, it could possibly be the fuel pump in the tank.<P>My father had something similar to this with his 89 Chevy truck not too long ago.  Idled ok, but anytime you gave it gas it would stumble and acted like it wanted to stall.  Found it was a faulty fuel pump causing it.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tigger.gif">           <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:RacingStripes@hotmail.com">RacingStripes@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||10-16-2000||11:54 AM||||I set the timing using the average of cylinders 1 and 4, per Haynes manual, while the computer was in diagnostic mode.<P>I considered fuel pump, but didn't know if it could cause the engine to stumble without a load on the engine. It will stumble in neutral, or when under acceleration.<P>I will head out today, and buy a guage. I triple checked the MAP lines, even replaced the "T" that plugs into the plenum from the MAP, since it was pretty well shot.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||10-23-2000||12:02 PM||||The problem remains.<P>I checked fuel pressure, it was low, so I replaced the fuel pump, strainer, pulsator, and filter. Fuel pressure is back up where it belongs, the gas tank is full, and it still stumbles under acceleration or when idling and I suddenly punch the throttle.<P>Plugs, wires, cap, rotor, fuel filter, fuel pump, TPS, MAP, IAC have all been replaced, along with checking, double checking, and triple checking all vacuume lines. <P>What is going on with this thing?||||reg||5
Z||000005||william_thorniii||10-23-2000||01:24 PM||||56,<P>Those sensors that you replaced... were they from the Formula, or the mystery engine?  Could it be possible that the sensors, more specifically the IAC and/or TPS could actually be malfunctioning?  I can head up there any day this week with my OTC scanner.  It could read the values of each sensor and then if we get my GT started, we could have some comparison values.  Give me a call at work today if you want me to come up tonight.  <P>William T. Thorn, III ||||reg||5
Z||000006||fiero56||10-23-2000||08:21 PM||||Sensors I have tried:<BR>off the SE, off the mystery engine, and off the Formula. Nothing changes between any of them, and the Formula doesn't have this problem. I might just get a decarbonizing kit from Ed and see if it changes anything, I've tried just about everything else... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||webbee||10-23-2000||11:18 PM||||You might try injector cleaner. I think the Chevron one with Technaline(?sp)is best. It might be a partially cloged injector(s) from sitting.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||10-23-2000||11:25 PM||||Could possibly be your EGR Valve. I dont think there very expensive. I had a bad one on my GT when I first got it, same type of symptoms you are having.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
